1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,968
Imagine that a copy of the platform's database leaves the bank on a stolen disk.

2
00:00:05,018 --> 00:00:08,856
The thief opens the table with Aziz's verified identity.

3
00:00:08,906 --> 00:00:12,728
What can he read?

4
00:00:12,778 --> 00:00:21,728
In Lesson 16, the platform kept Aziz's verified identity after one full proofing, with the reusable identifier from MyID next to it.

5
00:00:21,778 --> 00:00:30,248
This lesson shows how that record is protected: who holds its keys, who may read it, and how every read leaves a trace.

6
00:00:30,298 --> 00:00:32,480
First, what counts as protected.

7
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:45,032
The verified identity, in all its versions: names, date and place of birth, the personal identification number, gender, citizenship, nationality, document data and registered addresses.

8
00:00:45,082 --> 00:00:46,928
The phone number that MyID gave.

9
00:00:46,978 --> 00:00:50,360
The reusable identifier, and its expiry date.

10
00:00:50,410 --> 00:00:54,728
And the images from the camera, but only if the bank turns image storage on.

11
00:00:54,778 --> 00:00:56,864
Nothing else lives in this class.

12
00:00:56,914 --> 00:01:02,384
Phone numbers, devices and network addresses are personal data too, but other rules handle them.

13
00:01:02,434 --> 00:01:04,256
Now the keys.

14
00:01:04,306 --> 00:01:06,968
Each identity record has its own data key.

15
00:01:07,018 --> 00:01:14,528
The record is encrypted with that key, and the key itself is stored only in wrapped form, locked by the installation key.

16
00:01:14,578 --> 00:01:17,576
The installation key never leaves the bank's Vault.

17
00:01:17,626 --> 00:01:25,880
When the platform must read the record, it sends the wrapped key to Vault, gets it unwrapped for that one request, and forgets it when the request ends.

18
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:32,792
No part of the system keeps a decrypted copy longer than the request, and the identity never goes to a log.

19
00:01:32,842 --> 00:01:38,648
So the thief with the disk reads only ciphertext, and a wrapped key that he cannot open.

20
00:01:38,698 --> 00:01:47,560
But then, how does the platform find Aziz by his personal identification number, without decrypting every row?

21
00:01:47,610 --> 00:01:49,432
With a blind index.

22
00:01:49,482 --> 00:01:56,272
Next to the record, the platform keeps a keyed digest of the number, made with a second key from Vault: the index key.

23
00:01:56,322 --> 00:02:00,040
To find a person, it makes the same digest and looks it up.

24
00:02:00,090 --> 00:02:07,240
It is fast, it allows one active customer for each number, and without the index key the digest tells the thief nothing.

25
00:02:07,290 --> 00:02:12,736
Inside the gateway, only one module may touch this table: the identity module.

26
00:02:12,786 --> 00:02:14,608
Every other module asks it.

27
00:02:14,658 --> 00:02:19,048
And it answers with masked values, unless the purpose is on a short list.

28
00:02:19,098 --> 00:02:20,920
Here is that list.

29
00:02:20,970 --> 00:02:22,936
Keeping a new proofing result.

30
00:02:22,986 --> 00:02:27,064
The confirmation screen, on the device of the session that gave the result.

31
00:02:27,114 --> 00:02:28,936
The phone check.

32
00:02:28,986 --> 00:02:32,488
The customer lookup, with the personal identification number only.

33
00:02:32,538 --> 00:02:36,040
A case or a phone review, with masked values only.

34
00:02:36,090 --> 00:02:39,568
A compliance export, when a customer asks for his own data.

35
00:02:39,618 --> 00:02:41,440
The deletion job.

36
00:02:41,490 --> 00:02:46,000
And a verified address, when Aziz approves a sharing request.

37
00:02:46,050 --> 00:02:48,664
Now look at the other side: who never gets it.

38
00:02:48,714 --> 00:02:58,696
The bank's apps get only an identity summary, with the level and the display name, but no personal identification number, no document number, no address and no image.

39
00:02:58,746 --> 00:03:03,952
The bank's antifraud system, the notifications and the audit trail never get it.

40
00:03:04,002 --> 00:03:08,896
And a different app never gets it, except the address that Aziz chose to share.

41
00:03:08,946 --> 00:03:12,952
Every release is an audit event, with the reader and the purpose.

42
00:03:13,002 --> 00:03:16,240
Last month, an operator opened a case about Aziz.

43
00:03:16,290 --> 00:03:23,632
She saw only masked values: the first two and last two digits of his number, and the last four characters of his document.

44
00:03:23,682 --> 00:03:27,208
The audit trail recorded her, the purpose, and the time.

45
00:03:27,258 --> 00:03:30,064
I think this line matters more than the encryption.

46
00:03:30,114 --> 00:03:36,208
Encryption stops the thief with the disk, and the audit line stops the person who already has access.

47
00:03:36,258 --> 00:03:41,248
It also means that a later phase can show Aziz who read which data, when, and why.

48
00:03:41,298 --> 00:03:44,008
One more rule keeps the history honest.

49
00:03:44,058 --> 00:03:49,672
Identity data changes only through a refresh, which is a new full proofing.

50
00:03:49,722 --> 00:03:54,712
A refresh writes a new version, and keeps the old one for the configured retention time.

51
00:03:54,762 --> 00:03:59,776
The personal identification number is the same in every version of one customer.

52
00:03:59,826 --> 00:04:13,408
To recap: one protected class, a key for each record, the installation key in the bank's Vault, a blind index for search, one module at the door, masked reads by default, and an audit event for every release.

53
00:04:13,458 --> 00:04:17,632
One field in that record still needs a check: the phone number from MyID.

54
00:04:17,682 --> 00:04:19,696
It may be an old number from years ago.

55
00:04:19,746 --> 00:04:22,648
Does Aziz's phone number really belong to Aziz?

56
00:04:22,698 --> 00:04:24,616
Next: the phone check.
